### 8.6 Accumulating evidence from adopters without overclaiming

Per default-branch run Scout stores (SQLite and `signals/inertia.jsonl` in `scout/state`): snapshot revision/time, window bounds, scope ID, population digest, map version and scope lineage (split/merge), every §8.2/§8.3 value with numerator, denominator, tool/config digests, import-resolution coverage, artifact revision/producer, missingness reason, outcome maturity (right-censoring), and a compact sanitized change table (deduplicated change/PR keys → scope, churn, files/scopes touched, timestamps, workflow type, bot [-flag,-]{+flag and `bot_reason` (§8.3),+} linkage completeness, optional task category, human/agent provenance only when the repository supplies it). Medians alone cannot support re-analysis; the change table makes alternative outcome definitions possible later. Bulky evidence stays in artifacts.

`scout export --study` is a **separate opt-in** from local collection: schema in `docs/evidence/study-export.md`; repository-scoped pseudonyms; scope-level aggregates plus the change table; no source, commit messages, emails or reviewer identities; retention and withdrawal stated. Public `scout/state` branches carry a notice that git history makes erasure hard. Overlapping scopes are flagged so one change never multiplies pooled observations. Ownership is stored as coarse aggregates, never a personnel ranking.

